00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:31.740
(THE NORWEGIAN EXCEPTION)
 
Norway is currently the world's largest exporter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:36.420
of salmon, exporting to more than 100 countries.&nbsp;
But it is not only salmon that is exported. Norway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:41.580
is a major producer of dinosaur juice and gas.&nbsp;
Although we all love salmon, it is hydrocarbons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:47.160
that account for 40-70% of Norway's exports, and&nbsp;
which have made Norway such a wealthy country.
 &nbsp;

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:52.140
It was not easy to find oil in Norway. During the&nbsp;
1960s, they drilled more than 200 boreholes until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:56.760
they found a deposit: the EKOFISK oil field. And&nbsp;
I know what many of you are thinking! Especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:01.260
those of you who are already familiar with&nbsp;
VisualEconomik, our channel devoted to economics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:05.940
A country discovers oil? Watch out! They could&nbsp;
fall into the curse of natural resources! That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:10.740
right! Having a lot of natural resources does&nbsp;
not mean you will become rich. In many cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:14.580
the opposite is true. But we explain&nbsp;
this in detail in a VisualEconomik video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:19.380
which I’ll link for you in the description.
 
However, Norway is neither Russia nor Venezuela.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:29.340
It is a very special case. Listen up.
In 1972, Norway created the state-owned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:34.740
oil company STATOIL, now called EQUINOR. In&nbsp;
2001 Stateoil delisted part of its shares on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:39.300
the Oslo and New York stock exchanges, but&nbsp;
the government still held 81.7% ownership.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:44.220
Then in 2007 came a merger with the oil&nbsp;
and gas division of Norsk Hydro, Norway's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:49.380
leading hydropower producer. Right now, the&nbsp;
Norwegian State owns just over 68% of Equinor.
 &nbsp;

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:52.980
Naturally, this company distributes dividends...&nbsp;
which are mostly collected by the Norwegian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:56.820
government. Of course, that's not the only public&nbsp;
revenue stream. The Norwegian government also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:01.200
co-owns many of the country's oil and gas fields,&nbsp;
while taxes on profits from the exploitation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:06.360
crude oil are extremely high. In particular, the&nbsp;
State can keep more than 75% of the profits. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:10.680
other words, when it comes to oil in Norway,&nbsp;
the biggest beneficiary is always the State.
 &nbsp;

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:16.500
And the question is, what have&nbsp;
they done with all that money?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:25.920
Well, in 1990, Norway created the so-called&nbsp;
NORWEGIAN SOVEREIGN FUND: a State investment fund&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.960
for the purpose of investing surplus oil and gas&nbsp;
revenues. And why, you may ask? Well, because this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:35.700
sovereign wealth fund cushions all crises: Oil&nbsp;
price drops? No problem: Norway has investments in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:40.200
all kinds of assets. Assets that, of course, have&nbsp;
nothing to do with oil. This fund is managed by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:43.680
division of the Norwegian central bank.
 
What may surprise many of you is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:46.860
Norway's sovereign wealth fund is the&nbsp;
largest sovereign wealth fund in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:51.120
world alongside China's main fund and Abu&nbsp;
Dhabi's fund. Take a look at this chart.&nbsp;

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:58.500
To give you an idea, in 2013 the fund&nbsp;
represented the equivalent of 183% and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:02.580
in 2022 it already represents&nbsp;
more than 225% of their GDP.

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:05.100
But not only that, unlike state-owned programs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:08.880
in other countries, this fund has had&nbsp;
almost constant growth. They have even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:12.480
made significant returns in the wake of&nbsp;
the COVID pandemic and low oil prices.
 &nbsp;

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:16.080
But how much oil does Norway produce,&nbsp;
you may ask? Well, not very much.
 &nbsp;

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:26.520
Globally, Norway produces approximately&nbsp;
2% of the oil consumed and 3% of the gas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:29.700
This is far behind the production of&nbsp;
countries such as the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:33.120
Saudi Arabia, Russia, Canada&nbsp;
and Brazil, among many others.
 &nbsp;

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:38.220
To give you an idea, in 2021, Saudi Arabia&nbsp;
produced around 9 million barrels of oil per day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:44.040
the United States 11 million, Canada 4.7&nbsp;
million and Norway about 1.7 million barrels.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:49.440
However, Norway is the third largest exporter of&nbsp;
gas in the world, behind only Russia and Qatar.
 &nbsp;

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:56.160
In any case its point of difference, its&nbsp;
secret in respect to other countries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:59.580
has been based on being one of the few&nbsp;
countries that invests in the stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:03.900
market and diversifies its profits from oil and&nbsp;
gas. In this way the government is practically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:07.620
guaranteeing the future of Norwegians.
 
And the fact is that, as a rule,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:11.760
governments are net debtors. That is, they have&nbsp;
debts with the rest of the world. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:16.320
in the case of the United States, the public&nbsp;
debt is equivalent to some $93,000 dollars per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.940
US citizen; in the case of Italy it is equivalent&nbsp;
to more than $50,000; and in the case of Spain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:25.560
it is equivalent to more than $30,000.
 
Well, in Norway the situation is completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:29.940
different. Do you know how much each Norwegian&nbsp;
would pay in public debt? Absolutely nothing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:33.300
They could pay off the entire public debt&nbsp;
of the country with the money from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:38.940
Sovereign Fund and still have more than&nbsp;
$180,000 left over for each Norwegian.
 &nbsp;

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:46.080
Both right-wing and left-wing parties have&nbsp;
been conservative with the fund and have not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:49.980
squandered it. Something difficult to imagine&nbsp;
in other countries where coming to power often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:53.400
means sharing the spoils in order to win&nbsp;
votes. The first withdrawal of money from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:58.980
the sovereign wealth fund came in 2016, when&nbsp;
$780 million was spent to counter an economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:03.420
recession caused by low oil prices. In this&nbsp;
way, Norway barely noticed the oil shock.
 &nbsp;

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:07.200
To put it in another way. If there is a new&nbsp;
oil crisis that would make other producing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:11.640
countries suffer a lot, Norway could afford&nbsp;
to not even be touched by such a crisis.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:14.820
But do you want to hear a fact that&nbsp;
could make your head explode? In 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:19.380
the Norwegian government earned about $30&nbsp;
billion from oil exploitation in the country.
 &nbsp;

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:23.640
It turns out that the average return of the&nbsp;
Norwegian sovereign wealth fund, around 5.8%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:28.440
since its inception, means that in a typical year&nbsp;
the State "earns" more than twice as much from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:32.940
the fund's profits as it does from oil revenues&nbsp;
themselves. In other words, Norway is already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:36.960
more dependent on the fund than on oil.
 
N1 ("The 'big saving' that saves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:39.960
Norway from the oil crisis".
 
But beyond being very wealthy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:44.760
the question is, can Norway be the salvation of&nbsp;
a Europe heading for gasmageddon? We are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:52.380
to look at that right now.
 
T2 (NORWAY TO THE RESCUE?)
 &nbsp;

00:08:56.340 --> 00:09:00.840
Norway was already a key partner of the European&nbsp;
Union before the war in Ukraine. It exported 20%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:05.760
of Europe's natural gas supply. Up until last&nbsp;
year, Brussels was asking Oslo to stop drilling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:09.480
in the Arctic for ecological reasons.
 
Now, however, Brussels is asking Oslo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:14.100
to take oil and gas from wherever they can.
 
The goal is to replace Russian gas with more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:17.460
gas from Norway and other countries.
 
For the time being, Norway has already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:21.000
pledged to increase exports to Europe&nbsp;
in order to address the energy crisis.
 &nbsp;

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:26.520
N2 (Norway to raise hydrocarbon production&nbsp;
by 6.8% by 2023. – EuropaPress)
 &nbsp;

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:31.260
This increase is in addition to the increase&nbsp;
already seen in 2022. During this year Norway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:35.640
will produce 8% more natural gas than in&nbsp;
2021. In total the increase in oil and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:42.660
gas production will be almost 15% in 2021 and&nbsp;
6.8% in 2023. Almost 23% in just two years.
 &nbsp;

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:47.100
So if we're talking conspiracy theories again: can&nbsp;
you guess which European country has benefited the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:52.320
most from the Russian gas sanctions and the&nbsp;
sabotage of Nord Stream I? Exactly! Norway.
 &nbsp;

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:59.700
Coincidentally a new Norwegian gas pipeline was&nbsp;
put into operation on 1 October . The pipeline is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:04.140
called "Baltic" and has already started carrying&nbsp;
gas from Norway through Denmark to Poland.&nbsp;

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:08.340
The Baltic pipeline will transport&nbsp;
10 BCMs of gas per year to Poland.
 &nbsp;

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:13.200
Up until now Norway has been meeting 20% of&nbsp;
European gas demand, but this year it is estimated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:18.420
that it will already be meeting 25%. And next year&nbsp;
the percentage is expected to be even higher.
 &nbsp;

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:20.820
What’s more, the Norwegian&nbsp;
government itself says the following.&nbsp;

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:26.160
("They [Europe] will need gas from other sources&nbsp;
than Russia. And in that perspective, I think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:29.940
hard to see that there are many places where&nbsp;
it's more natural to continue to import gas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:35.220
from in a European context than from Norway.")
 
C2 (("For us, it's important that they know and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:39.660
show them that Norway is a reliable, trustworthy,&nbsp;
and long-term energy partner for Europe.")
 &nbsp;

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:44.580
(Andreas Bjelland Eriksen, State Secretary in&nbsp;
Norway's Ministry of Petroleum and Energy)
 &nbsp;

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:48.630
And EQUINOR has already said it will increase&nbsp;
production. But wait a minute! While Norway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.630 --> 00:10:51.600
is giving Europe a little breathing&nbsp;
space from its gasmageddon, it won't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.600 --> 00:11:01.140
be the ultimate solution for the time being.
 
Sometimes desire is not power. In Norway they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:04.620
are rubbing their hands with all the extra&nbsp;
income and want to sell more gas to a needy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:08.220
Europe. The problem? Norway can't double its&nbsp;
gas production overnight, and not only that!
 &nbsp;

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:11.760
Before the war, there were even those&nbsp;
who said that we should stop extracting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:16.080
hydrocarbons as soon as 2035. Okay, this&nbsp;
was the case of the Greens, but take note,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:20.640
because although the Labor Party itself, which is&nbsp;
now in government, did not set a specific date,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:25.080
it was in favor of negotiating it. In other&nbsp;
words, there was not much incentive to invest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:28.560
in the country's oil and gas industry.
 
In other words, it is impossible to turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:33.000
around years of disinvestment in a few months.
 
In addition, one thing that has made it difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:37.980
to buy gas in Europe is European regasification&nbsp;
capacity. A quick way to supply gas is to buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:42.240
liquefied natural gas. There would be no need to&nbsp;
wait to build new pipelines with Norway or other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:47.400
countries. But the EU's regasification capacity is&nbsp;
insufficient. Germany, which consumes the most gas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:52.200
in the EU, did not have a single regasification&nbsp;
plant before the Ukrainian war. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:56.640
Norway does not have sufficient liquefaction&nbsp;
capacity either. Because LNG has to be liquefied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:00.900
first and then regasified. You need capacity&nbsp;
at both ends of the transportation chain.
 &nbsp;

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:05.100
Put another way: Europe needs much more gas&nbsp;
than Norway can offer in the short term. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:09.240
other VisualPolitik videos we explain in&nbsp;
more detail what liquefied natural gas is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:13.500
and what the various alternatives are that&nbsp;
Europe is getting to try to counteract the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:18.120
energy crisis. Europe is diversifying its gas&nbsp;
purchases and preparing to receive liquefied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:21.660
natural gas from different countries.
 
But now the big question is: In Norway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:26.220
are they being supportive or is there some&nbsp;
vested interest behind it? Is there some dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:33.780
globalist agenda behind it all?
 
T3 (THE NORWEGIAN MONOPOLY)
 &nbsp;

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:41.160
As we said at the beginning of the video,&nbsp;
Norway is making a lot of money out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:45.780
war in Ukraine. They are making gold without&nbsp;
having to go looting like the Viking Olav.
 &nbsp;

00:12:45.780 --> 00:12:49.080
So what does this mean for Norway's sovereign&nbsp;
wealth fund and the global economy?
 &nbsp;

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:53.040
Well, watch out because in 2023 alone&nbsp;
the Norwegian government expects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:58.020
to earn more than $130 billion from the&nbsp;
exploitation of crude oil and natural gas.
 &nbsp;

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:03.480
And that's not all. At the moment, revenues&nbsp;
for 2022 are estimated at almost $120 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:07.020
And do you know how much they earned in&nbsp;
2021? A little less than 30 billion.
 &nbsp;

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:12.240
In other words, thanks to the war in Ukraine,&nbsp;
Norway will receive in 2022 and 2023 the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:15.960
equivalent income of eight regular years.
 
And do you know what? A large part of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:19.500
income will be earmarked for its sovereign&nbsp;
wealth fund, and this is no joke!
 &nbsp;

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:24.600
Norway already owns 1.3% of all shares&nbsp;
in the world. Companies such as Nestlé,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:29.040
Apple and Microsoft have the Norwegian Sovereign&nbsp;
Wealth Fund as one of their largest shareholders.
 &nbsp;

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:31.320
And stay tuned because here comes&nbsp;
the really interesting part!
 &nbsp;

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:35.580
One advantage of being a shareholder of a&nbsp;
company is that if you have enough shares,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:38.880
you can influence its direction.
 
Well, until a few years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:42.480
this Sovereign Wealth Fund did not pursue&nbsp;
any influence over the companies in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:46.380
it invested. Let's say that it limited itself&nbsp;
to excluding certain types of companies from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:51.480
its portfolio: basically arms manufacturers,&nbsp;
producers of hydrocarbons, tobacco, coal or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:55.440
companies with dubious links to human rights...&nbsp;
Something that of course it continues to do.
 &nbsp;

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:03.840
For example, the fund recently put Canada's&nbsp;
Bombardier and South Korea's Hyundai Glovis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:07.740
on a watch list for possible sale from&nbsp;
its investment. In the case of Hyundai,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:11.220
it was because of its alleged use of&nbsp;
scrapyards in Pakistan and Bangladesh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.220 --> 00:14:15.240
where labor conditions border on human rights&nbsp;
violations: at least, according to the fund.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:19.560
In the case of Bombardier, the reason was some&nbsp;
suspicions of corruption in various countries.
 &nbsp;

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:24.600
Then this same fund has also had controversies&nbsp;
with the Chinese government. They divested all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:28.440
shares in the sports company, Li-Ning,&nbsp;
due to an apparent violation of human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:34.680
rights in the Xinjiang region.
 
However, for some time now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:38.820
the fund's strategy has begun to change.
 
C3 ("We have evolved from being a reluctant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.820 --> 00:14:42.600
owner to a more active owner [...] In the&nbsp;
beginning, the idea was simply to buy a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:46.740
slice of the global financial markets and our&nbsp;
intention was not to exert any influence over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:51.180
the companies." Trond Grande, Deputy CEO&nbsp;
of the Norwegian Sovereign Wealth Fund)
 &nbsp;

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:55.500
For example, it is no longer unusual for this&nbsp;
fund to publish in advance how it is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:59.760
vote on certain issues at shareholder meetings&nbsp;
in order to generate pressure. They supposedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:03.720
do this to improve corporate governance.
 
C4 ("Our goal is to strengthen companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:07.980
through better governance and improve their&nbsp;
long-term risk management" Trond Grande, Deputy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:12.660
CEO of the Norwegian Sovereign Wealth Fund)
 
Along these lines, in 2021, the Norwegian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:17.460
fund voted in six resolutions at Facebook's&nbsp;
Shareholders' Meeting for Facebook to be more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:21.720
transparent and to present improved reporting.
 
However, right here is the potentially most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:25.920
disturbing issue: that from purely corporate&nbsp;
issues the Norwegian Fund moves on to pressuring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:29.700
companies with more cultural, identity&nbsp;
or political issues. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:33.660
that it tries to import Norwegian values&nbsp;
into the companies in which it invests.
 &nbsp;

00:15:33.660 --> 00:15:39.180
For example, in 2022, the Norwegian fund voted&nbsp;
in favor of a human rights report and a pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:42.480
gap report at Disney's shareholder meeting.&nbsp;
And something similar happened at Amazon.&nbsp;

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:50.700
Norway was on the side of Amazon workers&nbsp;
who wanted to set up a union. In fact, at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:55.260
a shareholders' meeting, Norway voted in favor of&nbsp;
collective bargaining rights against the interests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.260 --> 00:16:00.480
of Jeff Bezos himself. As a sidenote, I will tell&nbsp;
you that, in this case, Norway lost the vote.

00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:03.480
And it did so, among other reasons,&nbsp;
because the Fund's holdings in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:06.900
companies tend to be relatively small.
 
But of course, when you have so much money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:11.340
what you say within a company is, at the very&nbsp;
least, taken into account. And you can become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:15.600
a trend-setter. Many other funds and shareholders&nbsp;
may decide to copy you. To put it in a nutshell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:19.260
Norway in the stock market is like the&nbsp;
popular kid at school who sets trends.
 &nbsp;

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:23.160
And not only that, if necessary, this fund&nbsp;
could buy larger stakes in certain companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:26.880
in order to be able to influence them much&nbsp;
more. Believe me, with more than one trillion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:29.940
dollars in your investment portfolio&nbsp;
you can do almost anything you want.
 &nbsp;

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:33.600
Another example? Well, you see, Norway is not&nbsp;
only one of the richest countries in the world:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:36.120
it is also one of the most egalitarian. And it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:39.240
is precisely this social model that&nbsp;
they try to bring to their companies.

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:43.200
In fact, do you know what one of the major&nbsp;
causes Norway is fighting for? Exactly! The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:47.520
salary of managers: Norway has been pressuring&nbsp;
companies for years to lower the high salaries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:51.660
they pay to their managers. This means one thing:&nbsp;
the Norwegian sovereign wealth fund could end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:54.840
becoming a nightmare for CEOs.
 
That's why headlines like this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.840 --> 00:16:58.500
one don't come as a surprise:
 
N3("World's Largest Sovereign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.500 --> 00:17:02.160
Wealth Fund to Vote Against Apple CEO&nbsp;
Tim Cook's Pay Package". – Barrons)
 &nbsp;

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:05.340
And keep in mind the problem is that you&nbsp;
can start with the salary of managers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.340 --> 00:17:08.340
end up with many other initiatives.&nbsp;
Let's just say that if necessary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.340 --> 00:17:12.600
Norway could use the fund as a political&nbsp;
weapon... In one direction or the other.
 &nbsp;

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:19.740
This year, the Fund announced that it will&nbsp;
liquidate all its Russian assets. We are talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:24.120
about 2.8 billion dollars. It is not a large&nbsp;
sum if we compare it with the investments they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:27.780
have in other countries... But there it is.
 
And, logically, the more money the Fund has,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.780 --> 00:17:32.220
the more influence it has. So if one thing is&nbsp;
clear, it is that the huge torrent of money that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:36.600
is now pouring into the land of fjords will,&nbsp;
if anything, greatly increase that influence.
 &nbsp;

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:40.020
There are those who have harshly criticized&nbsp;
the Norwegian sovereign wealth fund for its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:44.340
behavior. Why? Because pursuing political&nbsp;
or ideological interests when investing can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:47.700
result in lost returns. And this could&nbsp;
be bad news for Norwegians themselves.
 &nbsp;

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:50.820
Of course, if we are talking about&nbsp;
criticism... Norway has also been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:54.840
strongly criticized for the large revenues&nbsp;
they are earning from the war. Poland's own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:58.560
prime minister has pointed out that Norway&nbsp;
is benefiting directly from the war and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:02.940
urged them to share this new wealth with Ukraine.
 
But... whatever the case, now the question is for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.940 --> 00:18:06.600
you: will Norway help Ukraine more&nbsp;
or will they seek to invest more in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:09.840
their sovereign wealth fund? Will Norway&nbsp;
manage to produce more gas to save Europe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:13.560
from the energy crisis? What will be the next&nbsp;
moves of the Norwegian sovereign wealth fund?

00:18:13.560 --> 00:18:15.900
You can leave me your answer&nbsp;
in the comments. As always,&nbsp;&nbsp;

